# Effects — Visual Node Editor for GPU Image Pipelines

## What This Is

A **monorepo (npm workspaces)** that ships three apps over a shared GPU-effects runtime:

- `apps/editor` — Tier-1 author-side **node editor** (React Flow + PixiJS). Wire processors; the engine executes the graph every frame in topological order. Toolbar Publish writes a `PublishedPipeline.json` for downstream consumers.
- `apps/supplier` — Tier-2 **supplier app**. Loads a `PublishedPipeline.json`, renders a curated form (image upload, simplified SAM segmentation, per-FieldDef widgets, tap-zone summary), runs the same `DataflowEngine` for live preview, exports `<effectId>-<timestamp>.config.json` for Tier-3. See `supplier-app.mdc`.
- `apps/player` — Tier-3 **web player demo**. Mounts `EffectPlayer` against pipeline + optional config; sidebar of EMIT buttons for `eventEmitter` nodes; native canvas hit-test for `tapZone` nodes. Integration acceptance test for any partner integration. See `player.mdc`.

All three consume three shared workspace packages:
- `packages/runtime` — framework-agnostic core: `DataflowEngine`, processor catalog, effect manifests, `pipeline/`, `publish.ts`, SAM worker. No React, no MUI, no @xyflow/react.
- `packages/player` — framework-agnostic Tier-3 web runtime: `EffectPlayer` facade + `applyOverrides` + canvas-sink + `SupplierConfig` + JSON I/O. No React.
- `packages/ui` — shared MUI widgets + dark `theme`. No engine deps.

> **Maintenance contract.** This document and `product-vision.mdc` are the canonical map of the codebase. When you change processor count, effect catalog, slot layout, persistence shape, directory layout, snapshot schema, animation pool semantics, or any other invariant — **update both files in the same change**. Out-of-date rules cost more debugging time than the documentation update. If you intentionally diverge from a rule, rewrite the rule.

